    feat(bundles): bundle + bundle_member tables and decision.scope (AUG-009)
    
    SPEC-002 section 1 ("The model") is now in the engine: the two bundle tables and the
    scope field on decisions, with the closed sets enforced and membership checked against
    the fleet rather than assumed.
    
    MODEL
    - COLS gains `bundle` (id, name, description, contract, status) and `bundle_member`
      (id, bundle_id, project, role, note); `decision` gains `scope` as its last column.
      Ids come from next_id(): B-000001 / BM-000001, fixed width like E-/F-.
    - BUNDLE_STATUSES (proposed|confirmed|retired) and BUNDLE_ROLES
      (owner|consumer|test-target) are closed sets refused BY NAME, the rule EDGE_KINDS
      already follows. Unknown values never reach a row.
    - `bundle()` / `bundle_member()` read like `edge()` / `facet()`: existence-checked
      writes. `bundle_member` refuses a bundle that does not exist, an unknown role, a
      project the fleet does not have, and a second row for the same (bundle, project) —
      a doubled member is a doubled blast radius in the section-2 walk.
    - Membership is verified against the scheduler's own `projects` table, READ-ONLY
      (sqlite3 uri mode=ro). AUGER_SCHEDULER_DB is an EXCLUSIVE override; otherwise the
      first candidate whose `projects` table can actually be read wins. The path named in
      the board row is a 0-byte file on this host, so the daemon's DB
      (~/.hermes/coding-hermes/scheduler.db) is the second candidate, and a 0-byte file is
      treated as an ABSENT answer rather than an empty fleet. With no readable scheduler DB
      at all, membership is refused with a "cannot verify" message naming the paths looked
      in: no sqlite exception escapes the write path, and nothing unverified is stored.
    - `decision_scope(row)` is the single reader of the default.
    
    CRITERION 3, HONESTLY: DEFAULT AND OLD NAMESPACES
    - The default scope is `project`, and it travels as an ABSENT key, never as the literal
      string: `auger answer` without --scope sends exactly the payload it always sent, so
      a namespace declared before this column keeps working untouched. Where the column IS
      declared, the namespace writer materialises it as JSON null — same meaning, and
      `decision_scope()` maps both to `project`.
    - DuckBrain never introspects or migrates a declared table, and its declared-table
      registry caches a namespace's declarations IN PROCESS on the first read of that
      namespace. So a namespace read before this change is SERVED a `decision` table with no
      `scope` column and refuses a row that carries one (400, "Unknown column(s) in insert").
      No migration is attempted — auger cannot add a column to a table the running server has
      already scanned. Instead: `insert_decision()` sends the row WITH the scope, and on that
      specific refusal retries without the key and prints a WARNING naming the decision, the
      downgrade (stored PROJECT-scoped, NOT bundle-scoped) and the remedy. The default path
      never reaches it, and a bundle scope is never lost silently.
    - Live answer for the shared `auger` namespace: `auger init` rewrote its declarations to
      the 13-table set, but the endpoint kept serving the 9-table listing held in the
      service's in-process cache (edge/facet were stale from an earlier tick too — this
      predates AUG-009). Nothing in production invalidates that cache, so the HTTP service
      was cycled with its unit's own lifecycle command; the API answered 200 again ~2s later.
      /api/ns/auger/tables now serves 13/13 tables, decision.scope included.
    
    GATE
    - bash tests/gate.sh -> GATE PASS. py_compile ok, `ruff check auger.py` clean, pytest
      45 passed (35 pre-existing + 10 new, with the live DuckBrain and live JEV arms
      running), tests/smoke.sh 15 passed / 0 failed including "all thirteen tables declared
      and visible". No live arm was skipped, so AUGER_CI=1 was not needed.
    - The 10 new cases cover all four acceptance criteria: the declarations as the API
      serves them and idempotent re-init; a bundle-scoped decision round-tripping with its
      scope on both the API projection and the namespace's own JSONL; the default scope and
      the unchanged default payload on disk; a member naming a real fleet project, a member
      naming one the fleet does not have (refused, nothing stored), the unverifiable-fleet
      path (override pointed at a missing path and at a 0-byte file, so it also runs where
      there is no fleet DB), and the pre-AUG-009 declaration path — a hand-written 3-table
      declaration written before the API is asked anything, which is the only way to make
      the server's first read see the old shape.
    - On a host with no fleet DB (a CI runner) the two membership cases skip loudly naming
      the remedy, as the board row requires; the refusal path itself is still exercised by
      the unverifiable-fleet case, which needs no fleet.
    
    Addresses AUG-009.
